<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles VII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1403">1403</a>–<a href="/years/1461">1461</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles the Victorious · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles VII was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1422. Helped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>, who raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">siege of Orléans</a> and saw him crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> in 1429, he recovered northern France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> from the English, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. He created a standing army and royal taxes, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 1444 and annexed Comminges to the crown in 1454.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-vii-of-france.jpg" alt="Charles VII of France" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KarlVII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born at the Hôtel Saint-Pol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 22 February 1403, the eleventh child and fifth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabeau-of-bavaria">Isabeau of Bavaria</a>. His four elder brothers each held the title of Dauphin and all died childless. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> seized Paris in 1418 the fifteen-year-old Charles became the Armagnac leader and set up his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a>. His men killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, on the bridge at Montereau during a meeting meant to reconcile them; how far Charles was involved has remained uncertain. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> of 1420 Charles VI recognised Henry V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as his heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In October 1422 northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, was ruled for the infant Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by the Duke of Bedford. Charles, mockingly called the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a>, kept an itinerant court in the Loire Valley. Joan of Arc reached him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinon">Chinon</a> on 23 February 1429, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">siege of Orléans</a> was lifted on 8 May, the French won at Patay on 18 June, and Charles was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 17 July 1429. Joan was captured by Burgundian troops in 1430 and burnt at the stake on 30 May 1431; Charles took no action to save her.</p><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1435">Treaty of Arras</a> of 5 August 1435 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> abandoned the English alliance. Charles entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 13 April 1436. He took royal control of church appointments through the Pragmatic Sanction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a> in 1438 and, between 1445 and 1448, formed a cavalry force 12,000 strong, followed by conscripted infantry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. After a truce at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> in 1444 he resumed the war in 1449. The French won at Formigny on 15 April 1450 and Castillon on 17 July 1453, leaving only Calais in English hands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles's last years were spoiled by conflict with his heir, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>, whom he banished to the Dauphiné in 1446 and who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> in 1456. Ill from 1458, Charles became convinced that traitors surrounded him, and died at Mehun-sur-Yèvre on 22 July 1461. His reputation has long been overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>, but he achieved what four generations of his predecessors had not, the expulsion of the English apart from Calais. He also founded the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 1432.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles VII of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Recovery came with Joan of Arc's relief of Orléans in 1429 and with <b>Charles VII</b>'s reforms, which in 1445 created the compagnies d'ordonnance, the first permanent royal cavalry, and used artillery to win at Formigny in 1450 and Castillon in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The comital line ended with Countess Marguerite, whose disputed inheritance was settled in favour of the crown; <b>Charles VII</b> annexed Comminges to the Kingdom of France in 1454.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-comminges">County of Comminges</a></li><li><blockquote>Metz fought the dukes of Lorraine and withstood a siege by <b>Charles VII</b> and René of Anjou in 1444.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-metz">Free Imperial City of Metz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Mehun-sur-Yèvre, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1440" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles VII of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1440">What was here in 1440? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
